-- A THIRD variant of examples/mem_disjoint_affine.tr's `m[i]`/`m[i+1]`
-- pair, driving stage 4 of DESIGN.md's dependent-type-system rollout
-- (`schedule.rs`: one generic disjointness query"): same shape, but
-- `m`'s depth is 10 -- NOT a power of two. The OLD eight hand-built
-- disjointness arguments (`schedule.rs`'s retired `IndexForm`/`forms_
-- differ`) required the mem's depth to be an EXACT power of two before
-- the same-base-affine argument would even fire, so this exact pattern
-- was UNPROVABLE before stage 4, purely because of the depth's own
-- factorization -- not because `i`/`i+1` were any less obviously
-- different addresses.
--
-- The new Z3 query (`bounds::provably_disjoint_mem_indices`) has no such
-- requirement: `i` and `i+1`, truncated to the mem's own real address
-- width (`clog2(10) = 4` bits), are provably different bit patterns for
-- EVERY value `i` can take -- including the wraparound case, `i = 15`'s
-- own `i+1 = 0` (mod 16) -- regardless of whether that address happens
-- to name a real cell (`0..10`) or the undefined padding region
-- (`10..16`, `v0` has no bounds check against it, left entirely to
-- `firtool`). The disjointness claim is purely about the two computed
-- ADDRESS PORT values never coinciding, which is exactly what matters
-- for a same-cycle read/write hazard -- not about whether either access
-- is itself in range.
--
-- Confirmed empirically: `--explain-schedule` reports no stall between
-- `write` and `read` at all, with no `conflict_free` annotation needed --
-- see `mem_disjoint_v2_non_power_of_two_depth_is_now_provable_via_the_
-- stage_4_z3_query`, tests/schedule.rs, for the same fact pinned as a
-- unit test.
module MemDisjointNonPowerOfTwo {
    mem m : [8][10]

    in i : [4]
    in x : [8]
    out y : [8] = 0

    rule write {
        m[i] := x
    }

    rule read {
        y := m[i+1]
    }

    schedule {
        urgency write > read
    }
}
